course: everyday
lesson: 255
title: Tests, exams and mistakes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When a test is announced, revising (going over what you learned) a little on several different days works better than cramming (trying to learn it all at once) the night before, because spread-out practice helps the brain remember for longer.
explain: In the test you write your name, read every question carefully and do the ones you know first; if you get stuck, you move on and come back at the end.
explain: Your eyes stay on your own paper, even when the teacher leaves the room, because a test must show what you know and honesty matters most when nobody is watching.
explain: A low mark is a map of what to practise next, not a measure of how clever you are forever, and you never change a mark or forge a signature to hide it.
explain: Everybody makes mistakes, even teachers; a girl in Perth who knocks over a glass jar tells the teacher straight away, so it can be cleaned up safely, and she is respected for owning up.
fact: Revising a little on several days helps you remember for longer than revising only the night before.
fact: In a test you write your name, read every question carefully and do the questions you know first.
fact: If you get stuck on one question, you move on and come back to it at the end, so there is time for the others.
fact: Looking at a neighbour's paper is cheating, because the test must show what you know.
fact: When the teacher leaves the room during a test, you keep working silently on your own paper.
fact: National tests, such as SATs in England or NAPLAN in Australia, also check how well the school teaches, and one test does not decide your future.
fact: A lower mark shows what to practise next; you look at what you got wrong and ask how to improve.
fact: Changing a mark or forging a parent's signature (writing their name yourself and pretending they did) is dishonest, and the trouble is much bigger when it is found out.
fact: Mistakes are how everyone learns; you cross a mistake out neatly or fix it and try again.
fact: If a teacher makes a mistake on the board, you politely put up your hand and say you think there may be an error.
fact: If you break something in the classroom, you tell the teacher straight away so it can be cleaned up safely.
fact: If you are blamed for a classmate's mistake, you calmly explain what really happened.
exercise: A spelling test is announced for next Friday. What is the best way to revise? | A little on several days before the test | All of it on Thursday night | Only on the morning of the test | Not at all, so your mind stays fresh
exercise: Why is revising on several days better than only the night before? | Spreading it out helps you remember for longer | Teachers can check which days you revised | Your brain only works well early in the week, on Mondays | Words learned at night are always forgotten
exercise: You are handed a test paper. What do you do first? | Write your name and read every question | Start the hardest question straight away to get it done | Wait until the others start writing, then copy their pace | Turn to the last page and begin there, working backwards
exercise: You are stuck on question 4 of a maths test. What is wise? | Move on and come back to it at the end | Keep working on it until you get it, however long it takes | Leave the test and give it in early | Ask your neighbour for the answer
exercise: Your neighbour's answers are easy to see in a test. What do you do? | Keep your eyes on your own paper | Check your answers against theirs | Copy only the answers you are unsure of | Cover their paper with your hand so they cannot see
exercise: The teacher leaves the room for a minute during a test. What is right? | Keep working silently on your own paper | Quickly ask a friend for an answer | Look in your book while she is away | Stop working until she comes back
exercise: Why do national tests such as NAPLAN or SATs happen? | They also check how well the school teaches | They decide which job you will have | They decide whether you can stay at school | They are given only to the cleverest children
exercise: You get 12 out of 20, lower than you hoped. What is the best next step? | Look at what you got wrong and ask how to improve | Decide you are bad at the subject and always will be | Throw the paper away before anyone sees it, then forget about it | Blame the teacher for setting a test that was far too hard
exercise: You want to change a 3 into an 8 on your test before your parents see it. What is right? | Never change it; show the test honestly | Change it neatly so nobody can tell | Sign the test yourself in your parent's name | Lose the test on the way home
exercise: You make a mistake in your class work. What do you do? | Cross it out neatly or fix it, and try again | Tear out the whole page and hide it in your bag | Stop working because it is spoiled | Scribble over it until nobody can see it
exercise: The teacher writes 7 × 8 = 54 on the board. What do you do? | Put up your hand and politely say there may be an error | Shout out that the teacher is wrong | Say nothing, because teachers are never wrong | Laugh with your friends about it
exercise: You accidentally break a glass beaker in the science corner. What do you do? | Tell the teacher straight away | Pick up the pieces yourself and hide them | Walk away quickly and hope that nobody saw | Blame the child sitting next to you
exercise: The teacher thinks you spilled the paint, but it was a classmate. What should you do? | Calmly explain what really happened | Shout that it was not you and cry | Say nothing and take the blame | Spill more paint on the classmate's work
exercise: Why should you not spend the whole test on one hard question? | It leaves no time for the other questions | Hard questions are worth no marks at all | Teachers take marks off for every minute of slow work | The hard question is always a trick
test: Ben crams all his times tables on Sunday night, and Ava practises ten minutes a day all week. Who will probably remember them longer? | Ava, because spread-out practice lasts longer | Ben, because he learned them most recently of the two | Both the same, as the time spent on the tables is similar | Ben, because one long session goes deeper into the brain
test: In a reading test, a boy in Leeds reaches a question he cannot answer. Twenty minutes are left and six questions remain. What should he do? | Skip it, answer the rest and come back at the end | Stay on it until the time runs out | Give in the paper without the rest | Write any answer and never come back
test: Why is it cheating to copy even when the teacher has left the room? | The test must show what you know, whoever is watching | It is only cheating if the teacher sees it | Copying is allowed when you are stuck and the teacher is out | The teacher will check the cameras later
test: A girl in Brisbane is so worried about NAPLAN that she cannot sleep. What is true? | One test does not decide her future; her best is enough | If she does badly she will have to leave school | NAPLAN alone decides which high school she will go to next year | She should stay home on the test day
test: Leo forges his mum's signature on a bad test, and the teacher phones home. What happens? | The trouble is much bigger than the bad mark would have been | Nobody minds, because the mark was bad anyway | The teacher raises his mark | His mum is pleased he did not worry her
test: What does a low mark really tell you? | What you need to practise next | That you will never be good at the subject | That the teacher does not like you | That you should stop trying, because tests are unfair
test: Your teacher spells "necessary" wrongly on the board. Which response is best? | Raise your hand and kindly say there may be a spelling error | Tell the whole class she cannot spell | Copy the wrong spelling down, to be polite to the teacher | Walk up and write the right spelling on the board yourself, without asking
test: A boy in Ottawa knocks a plant pot off the window sill and soil spills everywhere. Why should he tell the teacher at once? | It can be cleaned safely, and owning up is respected | He will be given a reward for breaking it | Otherwise the plant will grow back wrong and crooked | The teacher can find somebody else to blame for the mess
test: Your partner knocked over the paint, but the teacher thinks it was you. Which is best? | Tell the teacher calmly what really happened | Stay silent and get angry later | Tell the teacher your partner is a liar | Say it was you, just to keep things quiet
test: test tmrw, gonna cram it all tonight. best way? | No, a little on several days works better | Yes, the night before is best, it's fresh in your head | Don't revise at all | Revise only at breakfast
test: stuck on question 3, been on it ages | Move on and come back at the end | Stay on it till the bell, you'll crack it eventually | Look at your neighbour's answer | Leave the rest blank
test: got a bad mark, could i just sign mum's name? | No, that's dishonest, show her the mark | Yes, if it looks just like hers | Yes, she'd sign anyway | Only on a small test
test: teacher wrote 3 x 4 = 7 on the board?? that's wrong innit | Put your hand up and politely say there may be a mistake | Shout that she's wrong | Copy it down, teachers are always right about sums | Laugh about it with your friends at the back of the class
explain: A boy in Ohio stayed up nearly all night cramming before a big test; next morning he was so tired he could not remember things he knew well, and he did worse than in practice.
explain: A girl in England hid a run of bad marks from her parents and teacher; by the time anyone found out, she was a long way behind and needed months of extra help.
fact: Sleep helps the brain store what you learned, so staying up all night usually makes results worse.
fact: Hiding mistakes and bad marks stops people from helping you while the problem is still small.
test: big test tmrw, gonna stay up all night revising | Revise a bit, then sleep; sleep helps memory | Stay up, the more hours you put in the better you do | Sleep 2 hours, that's plenty | Drink cola all night to stay sharp
test: Why is telling a parent about a bad mark better than hiding it? | They can help before you fall further behind | They will never find out anyway | Bad marks disappear if hidden | Teachers prefer you keep it quiet
test: got 3 outta 20, gonna bin it before mum sees | Show her and ask for help with what went wrong | Bin it, she'll never know | Change it to an 8 with a pen that looks the same | Say the teacher lost it
test: did u hear a girl changed her test mark from a 4 to a 9 and her mum found out and it was way worse than the 4? real lesson? | Changing a mark is dishonest and makes trouble bigger | Use a pen that matches the teacher's better | Only change marks by a little, so nobody notices it | Marks don't matter so it's fine
test: apparently if u fail the SATs u can't go to secondary school, my cousin said. probably just a rumour? | Yes; one test doesn't decide your future | No, it's the rule in every school in the country | No, you repeat the whole year | No, you have to change school
test: my mate says he stayed up all night revising and smashed the test, so that's the way. right? | No, sleep helps you remember; all-nighters usually hurt | Yes, all-nighters always work | Yes, sleep wastes revision time you could be using | Yes, if you drink cola and keep the lights on all night long
exam: Identify the most effective pattern of revision for long-term remembering. | Revising a little on several days | Revising only on the night before | Revising for a whole day once | Reading the book once quickly
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate response upon becoming stuck on a test question? | To move on and return to it at the end | To remain on it until the time expires | To copy the answer of a neighbour | To leave the remaining questions blank
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a lower mark. | It indicates what is to be practised next | It proves that one cannot learn the subject | It should be hidden from one's parents | It means the test was unfair
exam: Ascertain the proper conduct where a teacher has made an error upon the board. | To raise one's hand and politely say there may be an error | To laugh loudly at the teacher | To tell the class at break time that the teacher was wrong | To correct it oneself upon the board without permission
